# Use Case 3: Pipeline Leak Detection & Integrity Agent

## Segment: Midstream | Amazon Bedrock AgentCore

---

## The Problem

A midstream operator manages 200 miles of natural gas transmission pipeline with 8 SCADA measurement stations. Leaks manifest as pressure drops and flow imbalances between adjacent stations, but distinguishing a real leak from normal operational transients (compressor starts, valve changes, temperature-driven line pack shifts) requires contextual reasoning that rule-based SCADA alarms cannot do.

The result is a lose-lose situation: set the alarm threshold too sensitive and you get constant false alarms that cause unnecessary shutdowns ($100K+ per event) and alarm fatigue. Set it too conservative and you miss real leaks. Over 40% of pipeline leaks in remote areas go undetected for more than a week, a delay that can escalate repair costs by 600% while risking environmental disasters.

DOT PHMSA penalties for pipeline incidents reach up to $2.7M per violation. Modern AI-driven leak detection systems achieve 98% accuracy in pinpointing leaks and reduce detection time from days to minutes. The challenge is not just detection, it is intelligent disambiguation: knowing when an anomaly is a real leak vs. a compressor transient vs. a cold morning causing line pack contraction. That requires an agent that reasons in context, not just a threshold alarm.

## The Solution

Build an autonomous pipeline integrity agent using **Amazon Bedrock AgentCore** that monitors SCADA telemetry, reasons about anomalies in full operational context, localizes potential leak segments, assesses severity, and orchestrates the incident response workflow while maintaining persistent memory of the pipeline's normal operating behavior so it can distinguish real events from noise.

The agent reasons like an experienced pipeline controller. When it sees a pressure drop, it does not just fire an alert. It checks whether a compressor started recently, fetches live weather data to correct for temperature-driven line pack changes, runs a mass balance calculation across all stations, estimates the leak rate and location using pipeline physics, checks the regulatory reporting threshold, and drafts a complete incident notification with recommended isolation actions. The control room operator gets a full diagnostic package in under 5 minutes, not a raw alarm.

